{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/wireparity at 3a45e1f256185399852b20a3695833a93812b5b8 — the TypeScript mirror gate extended to the review projection, under ratified proposal c26a7f57 (25981e55). The thread rail computes its salience from projection.reviews rather than by scanning report text, because the fold already knows which verdict is the latest effective one and a text scanner would have named the wrong head in the acceptance thread, where three of four verdicts are superseded rounds and two are ratified approvals. Reading that projection means ui/src/lib/api.ts now mirrors workroom.Review, and an unmirrored wire type is exactly the gap this gate exists to close — its own comment says adding a wire type without adding it here is the one gap it cannot close by itself. internal/wireparity/parity_test.go therefore adds \"Review\": workroom.Review{} to the mirrored map beside Decision, Statement, Commitment, Artifact and Act, so a renamed or dropped json tag on Review fails the build rather than turning a working read into undefined that TypeScript cannot catch. Conditions actually met at this exact head: go test -count=1 ./internal/wireparity/ passes with the new pair, confirming api.ts names exactly the fields Go emits for Review — report, timestamp, reviewer, verdict, head, artifact, implementer, resolved_by, independence, ratified, retired, stale — and the five pre-existing pairs still match. go vet, go test -count=1 ./..., go test -race -count=1 ./..., and the docset gates all pass. Carried once onto main bbc81674; none of the intervening merges touched this package or internal/workroom's wire types. Predecessor at this exact path: b98612c4 (#4825) at 8924b4d0, which is world-stale, to be retired at merge.","body":{"commit":"3a45e1f256185399852b20a3695833a93812b5b8","path":"internal/wireparity"}}